WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
Many Python applications rely on an external identity provider to handle primary authentication and MFA.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:14.440
Single sign-on can improve user experience and centralize governance, but it also shifts some trust to identity assertions.

3
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:25.820
The application still needs to validate tokens, signatures or integrity, issuer, audience, expiration, and relevant claims according to the protocol and provider guidance.

4
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.360
It also still needs to enforce local authorization.

5
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.000
OpenID Connect concepts at a high level provide an identity layer for sign-in and identity claims.

6
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.060
SAML concepts support enterprise federation in many environments.

7
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:52.880
OAuth delegated access concepts are different: OAuth can authorize an application to access resources, but it should not be confused with authentication unless an identity layer and validation model are present.

8
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.580
Mature libraries and provider guidance should be used instead of hand-rolling federation behavior.

9
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.500
Claims may indicate authentication context or assurance at a high level.

10
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.120
For example, a provider may communicate whether MFA occurred or what kind of authentication was used.

11
00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:13.540
Python applications should not blindly trust any claim from any source.

12
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:21.320
They should validate the assertion, understand what the claim means for that provider, and decide whether local policy accepts it for the requested action.

13
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.060
Ambiguous claims should not silently grant high-risk access.

14
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.980
APIs and service-to-service calls also need thoughtful identity design.

15
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.160
A human user's MFA status may not be relevant to a machine credential, and a service account may need separate controls.

16
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:44.860
Some applications may enforce local step-up even when SSO handles primary login, especially for sensitive local actions.

17
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:54.720
MFA is part of a broader identity system that includes SSO, sessions, tokens, delegated access, workload identity, and authorization.
